          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "Please discuss your blood pressure readings with a clinician, who can give you advice tailored to your personal health [research]. If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice [diagnostic].\n\nYour home readings of 150/95 mm Hg are consistently elevated [diagnostic]. Major cardiology society consensus guidance classifies a systolic blood pressure of 140 mm Hg or higher, or a diastolic blood pressure of 90 mm Hg or higher, as Stage 2 hypertension, which includes your readings [research, \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"]. It is important to know that a single high reading in a clinic is not itself a diagnosis of high blood pressure; diagnosis is based on an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions [diagnostic].\n\nFor most adults, United States guidelines suggest a blood pressure target below 130/80 mm Hg [diagnostic, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets]. This target is particularly recommended in the context of secondary prevention for coronary artery disease [research, \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\"]. However, specific blood pressure targets can be individualized based on factors like older age, other health conditions, and individual circumstances [diagnostic, research].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
